1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,240
After the SIM swap, Aziz buys a new SIM card.

2
00:00:03,290 --> 00:00:06,792
The operator gives him a number that someone else used for years.

3
00:00:06,842 --> 00:00:11,112
That someone is still a customer of the bank, and the number still sits on their account.

4
00:00:11,162 --> 00:00:15,216
He puts it into a new phone, and types the number into the mobile bank.

5
00:00:15,266 --> 00:00:22,488
To the platform, Aziz now looks exactly like a thief: a known number, a strange phone, and no password.

6
00:00:22,538 --> 00:00:26,376
How does the platform tell a new owner of a number from a thief of one?

7
00:00:26,426 --> 00:00:31,080
In Lesson 31, the number left Aziz's account after the SIM question.

8
00:00:31,130 --> 00:00:39,648
This lesson covers three ways that a number and a person drift apart: number reassignment, re-registration, and a dormant account.

9
00:00:39,698 --> 00:00:41,304
Start with the reassignment.

10
00:00:41,354 --> 00:00:45,648
The number belongs to an active customer, so this is not a registration.

11
00:00:45,698 --> 00:00:50,328
The mobile bank asks for that customer's password, and Aziz does not know it.

12
00:00:50,378 --> 00:00:54,720
So he taps Forgot password, and the reset runs the checks of a new phone.

13
00:00:54,770 --> 00:01:00,768
TAF sends a code, and it lands on Aziz's SIM card, because the number really is his now.

14
00:01:00,818 --> 00:01:05,064
The old owner was at level two, so the account check asks for MyID.

15
00:01:05,114 --> 00:01:08,400
MyID checks the face, and the face is Aziz's.

16
00:01:08,450 --> 00:01:13,824
The personal identification number from MyID belongs to a different person than the old owner.

17
00:01:13,874 --> 00:01:15,696
That is the signal.

18
00:01:15,746 --> 00:01:17,472
A thief fails the face.

19
00:01:17,522 --> 00:01:21,048
A new owner passes it, and turns out to be someone else.

20
00:01:21,098 --> 00:01:23,880
So the platform removes the number from the old owner.

21
00:01:23,930 --> 00:01:25,752
It does not block the old owner.

22
00:01:25,802 --> 00:01:29,832
They did nothing wrong: the operator simply gave their old number away.

23
00:01:29,882 --> 00:01:31,704
Their sessions end.

24
00:01:31,754 --> 00:01:38,328
A push message, number removed, goes to all their devices, and they verify a new number at their next login.

25
00:01:38,378 --> 00:01:43,968
The audit trail records the event number reassigned, and the antifraud system hears about it.

26
00:01:44,018 --> 00:01:47,592
I think this is the most important line in the lesson: never a block.

27
00:01:47,642 --> 00:01:51,168
A number changing hands is normal life, not fraud.

28
00:01:51,218 --> 00:01:53,040
Now, who gets the number?

29
00:01:53,090 --> 00:01:56,016
The platform looks at the person that MyID found.

30
00:01:56,066 --> 00:02:02,664
If that person has no account, and MyID lists this number for them, they become a new customer at level two.

31
00:02:02,714 --> 00:02:07,920
If MyID does not list it, they start at level one, with the number under a phone review.

32
00:02:07,970 --> 00:02:11,184
But Aziz is already a customer, with his old number.

33
00:02:11,234 --> 00:02:14,712
So the second rule applies: re-registration.

34
00:02:14,762 --> 00:02:19,248
Re-registration is for one person who arrives under a second number.

35
00:02:19,298 --> 00:02:21,288
The usual shape is simple.

36
00:02:21,338 --> 00:02:26,112
Someone registers a new number, at level one, and later steps up with MyID.

37
00:02:26,162 --> 00:02:30,696
MyID says: this person already exists, with a different number.

38
00:02:30,746 --> 00:02:33,264
Now the platform asks one question.

39
00:02:33,314 --> 00:02:36,744
Is the new number on my I D's list of numbers for that person?

40
00:02:36,794 --> 00:02:40,584
If yes, the platform moves the number to the existing customer.

41
00:02:40,634 --> 00:02:43,080
The new phone becomes the trusted device.

42
00:02:43,130 --> 00:02:48,720
Every earlier device of the customer is revoked, with the reason replaced by re-registration.

43
00:02:48,770 --> 00:02:50,952
The short level-one record is closed.

44
00:02:51,002 --> 00:02:55,680
And an SMS goes to the old number: a new phone was registered.

45
00:02:55,730 --> 00:02:59,712
Aziz's new number is on his MyID list, so this is his path.

46
00:02:59,762 --> 00:03:03,504
His new phone is now trusted, and his old phone is revoked.

47
00:03:03,554 --> 00:03:07,344
If the number is not on the list, the platform does nothing on its own.

48
00:03:07,394 --> 00:03:12,912
The step-up stops, the level-one customer stays as it was, and a case opens for an operator.

49
00:03:12,962 --> 00:03:18,600
Re-registrations are also limited per day, so nobody can move numbers around in a loop.

50
00:03:18,650 --> 00:03:20,472
The third case is about time.

51
00:03:20,522 --> 00:03:23,808
Suppose a customer does not touch the bank for a long while.

52
00:03:23,858 --> 00:03:27,816
None of the devices are used for longer than the configured dormancy period.

53
00:03:27,866 --> 00:03:29,448
That account is dormant.

54
00:03:29,498 --> 00:03:34,128
A dormant account never opens from a new phone with a one-time code alone.

55
00:03:34,178 --> 00:03:38,688
Old numbers get new owners, and nobody is watching the old sessions.

56
00:03:38,738 --> 00:03:40,512
So the platform asks for more.

57
00:03:40,562 --> 00:03:48,432
A customer who was ever at level two passes a MyID re-check, or a full proofing when the reusable identifier has expired.

58
00:03:48,482 --> 00:03:54,336
In a side app, the login starts at level one, and level two needs the approval in the master app.

59
00:03:54,386 --> 00:03:58,968
A dormant customer who was never at level two has no face on file.

60
00:03:59,018 --> 00:04:03,408
So the platform locks the old record, and makes a new customer at level one.

61
00:04:03,458 --> 00:04:06,720
The audit trail records a dormant account reset.

62
00:04:06,770 --> 00:04:08,952
Nothing is handed over on a code alone.

63
00:04:09,002 --> 00:04:13,680
And when MyID finds a different person here too, it is a number reassignment again.

64
00:04:13,730 --> 00:04:19,008
Each of these cases runs in one database transaction, together with its events.

65
00:04:19,058 --> 00:04:22,416
So a crash can never leave one number on two customers.

66
00:04:22,466 --> 00:04:27,144
To recap: a number belongs to a person only while MyID agrees.

67
00:04:27,194 --> 00:04:32,304
A new owner proves the face, and the number moves, with no block on the old owner.

68
00:04:32,354 --> 00:04:37,848
One person with two numbers is joined by re-registration, but only when MyID lists the number.

69
00:04:37,898 --> 00:04:40,608
And a dormant account needs more than a code.

70
00:04:40,658 --> 00:04:45,312
Aziz is settled again: one number, one trusted phone, level two.

71
00:04:45,362 --> 00:04:48,864
But his passport, the one MyID checked, expires next year.

72
00:04:48,914 --> 00:04:52,056
What happens to his level two when the document runs out?

73
00:04:52,106 --> 00:04:54,696
Next: the identity refresh.
